\caption(Interior phase means exclude all boundaries; divisible kernel size equalises counts but does not constrain learned phase weights.)
\alttext(Interior phase means exclude all boundaries; divisible kernel size equalises counts but does not constrain learned phase weights.)

> Checking the theory

Two batches, three fine channels and two coarse channels check both the bilinear identity and derivatives.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::Generator rng(18);
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  double worst = 0;
  for (auto padding : {n::Padding::Same, n::Padding::Valid}) {
    const int h = padding == n::Padding::Same ? 8 : 9,
              w = padding == n::Padding::Same ? 10 : 11;
    auto fine = n::normal({2, h, w, 3}, 0, .2, rng, type).requires_grad_();
    auto kernel = n::normal({3, 3, 3, 2}, 0, .2, rng, type).requires_grad_();
    const auto coarse = n::normal({2, 4, 5, 2}, 0, .2, rng, type);
    const auto options = n::conv_options(2, padding);
    const auto forward = n::conv2d(fine, kernel, options);
    const auto transpose = n::conv2d_transpose(coarse, kernel, fine.shape(), options);
    const auto from_grad = n::grad(n::sum(forward * coarse), {fine})[0];
    worst = std::max(
        {worst, std::abs(n::sum(forward * coarse).item() - n::sum(fine * transpose).item()),
         n::max(n::abs(from_grad - transpose)).item()});
    auto probe = n::normal({1, 2, 2, 1}, 0, .2, rng, type).requires_grad_();
    auto small = n::normal({2, 2, 1, 1}, 0, .2, rng, type).requires_grad_();
    const auto check = n::gradcheck(
        [](const std::vector<n::Tensor> &v) {
          return n::conv2d_transpose(v[0], v[1], n::conv_options(2, n::Padding::Valid));
        },
        {probe, small});
    if (!check.ok)
      return 1;
  }
  if (worst > 1e-10)
    return 1;
  std::print("SAME/VALID adjoint and input-gradient maximum error {:.12f}; transposed "
             "input/filter finite differences passed.\n",
             worst);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
SAME/VALID adjoint and input-gradient maximum error 0.000000000000; transposed input/filter finite differences passed.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The fine output agrees with the forward map's adjoint, including input/filter finite
differences. Checkerboards are visible on constant inputs, and the interior phase check
separates uneven overlap from padding boundaries and learned phase weights.

> References

- [Dumoulin and Visin (2016), A guide to convolution arithmetic for deep learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07285).
